        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">B. R. Ambedkar</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1891 CE – 1956 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">14 April 1891 in the town and military cantonment of Mhow (now Dr Ambedkar Nagar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>); the 14th and last child of Ramji Maloji Sakpal, a subedar in the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>, and Bhimabai Sakpal, of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> Mahar family from Ambadawe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratnagiri">Ratnagiri</a> district</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">6 December 1956 at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in his sleep, three days after finishing his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> The Buddha and His Dhamma; cremated in a Buddhist ceremony at Dadar Chowpatty beach on 7 December</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Professor of political economy at Sydenham College (from 1918); principal of the Government Law College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> (1935, for two years); founder of the Independent Labour Party (1936) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a> Federation; minister of labour on the Viceroy's Executive Council; Law Minister in Nehru's first cabinet (1947 to 1951); chairman of the Drafting Committee of the Constitution; member of the Rajya Sabha</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">campaigns against untouchability, including the Mahad satyagraha of 1927; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poona-pact">Poona Pact</a> of 1932; Annihilation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a> (1936); chairing the drafting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a>; writings on economics, including The Problem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">Rupee</a>; his conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in 1956 and the mass conversions that followed</span></div>
