    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born a Mahar, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> treated as untouchable, Ambedkar was segregated at school, yet went on to doctorates at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> School of Economics and was called to the Bar at Gray's Inn.</li><li>From 1927 he led marches, water and temple campaigns and the burning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manusmriti">Manusmriti</a> against untouchability, and in 1932 he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poona-pact">Poona Pact</a> with Madan Mohan Malaviya after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s fast against separate electorates.</li><li>He chaired the drafting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a>, resigned from Nehru's cabinet in 1951 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> code bill was defeated, and in 1956 converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> with some 500,000 followers.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>At school Ambedkar had to sit on a gunny sack that he took home with him, and when he needed water someone had to pour it for him from a height, which he later summed up as &quot;No peon, No Water&quot;. His personal library held more than 50,000 books.</p></aside>
    </section>

