    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1891</span><span class="rf-text">Born at Mhow, the 14th child of Ramji Maloji Sakpal, into a Mahar family</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1913</span><span class="rf-text">Awarded a Baroda State Scholarship to study at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1927</span><span class="rf-text">Begins active movements against untouchability, leading the Mahad satyagraha for the right to draw water from the town's main tank and, in December, burning copies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manusmriti">Manusmriti</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1932</span><span class="rf-text">Signs the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poona-pact">Poona Pact</a> with Madan Mohan Malaviya, giving the Depressed Classes reserved seats within the general electorate</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">Publishes Annihilation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a> and founds the Independent Labour Party</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1947</span><span class="rf-text">Becomes Law Minister in Nehru's cabinet and is appointed Chairman of the Drafting Committee of the Constitution</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1956</span><span class="rf-text">Converts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> and then converts some 500,000 of his supporters</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1956</span><span class="rf-text">Dies in his sleep at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> on 6 December</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar was born on 14 April 1891 at Mhow, a town and military cantonment now officially called Dr Ambedkar Nagar, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>. He was the 14th and last child of Ramji Maloji Sakpal, a subedar in the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>, and Bhimabai Sakpal. The family came from Ambadawe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratnagiri">Ratnagiri</a> district and belonged to the Mahar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, which was treated as untouchable and subjected to socio-economic discrimination. At school he and other untouchable children were segregated, were not allowed to sit inside the class and were given little help by teachers.</p><p>His father retired in 1894 and the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a> two years later; soon afterwards his mother died, and a paternal aunt cared for the children in difficult circumstances. Of his brothers and sisters only Ambedkar passed his examinations and went to high school. His surname in the records changed from Sakpal to Ambadawekar and then to Ambedkar, the name of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> teacher, Krishnaji Keshav Ambedkar.</p><p>In 1897 the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, where he was the only untouchable enrolled at Elphinstone High School. In 1906 he married Ramabai in a match arranged by their parents. He passed his matriculation in 1907 and entered Elphinstone College, becoming, by his own account, the first from his caste to do so, and by 1912 he held a degree in economics and political science from Bombay University.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;B. R. Ambedkar&#8221; cites where it covers Early life and education, Education:</p><ul><li>Shashi Tharoor, B. R. Ambedkar: The Man Who Gave Hope to India's Dispossessed (Manchester University Press, 2023)</li><li>Christophe Jaffrelot, Ambedkar and Untouchability: Fighting the Indian Caste System (Columbia University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii.jpg" alt="Sayajirao Gaekwad III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii">Sayajirao Gaekwad III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sayajirao_Gaekwad_III,_Maharaja_of_Baroda,_1919.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lafayette, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1913, a few weeks after his father's death, Ambedkar was awarded a Baroda State Scholarship under a scheme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii">Sayajirao Gaekwad III</a>, the Gaekwad of Baroda, for postgraduate study at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City. He passed his M.A. in June 1915, majoring in economics, and was influenced there by John Dewey. In 1916 he presented the paper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Castes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>: Their Mechanism, Genesis and Development to a seminar run by the anthropologist Alexander Goldenweiser, and in October of that year he enrolled at Gray's Inn and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> School of Economics.</p><p>He returned to India in 1917 when his scholarship ended, and went back to London in 1920 with support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahu-of-kolhapur">Shahu of Kolhapur</a>. He completed a master of science degree at the LSE in 1921, and in 1923 the University of London awarded him a D.Sc. in economics for The Problem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">Rupee</a>; the same year he was called to the Bar. His Columbia Ph.D. followed in 1927.</p><p>Back in India he struggled to make a living: an investment consulting business failed when clients learned he was an untouchable. In 1918 he became professor of political economy at Sydenham College, where other professors objected to his sharing a drinking-water jug with students. In 1920 he began the weekly Mooknayak with Shahu's help, and he later founded the Bahishkrit Hitakarini Sabha to promote education and welfare among the "outcastes".</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;B. R. Ambedkar&#8221; cites where it covers Political career:</p><ul><li>Christophe Jaffrelot, Dr Ambedkar and Untouchability: Analysing and Fighting Caste (C. Hurst &amp; Co. Publishers, 2005)</li><li>Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar, Valerian Rodrigues, The Essential Writings of B.R. Ambedkar (Oxford University Press, 2002)</li><li>H.V. Hande, Ambedkar &amp; the Making of the Indian Constitution: A Tribute to Babasaheb B.R. Ambedkar (Macmillan Publishers India, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi.jpg" alt="Mahatma Gandhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahatma-Gandhi,_studio,_1931.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elliott &amp; Fry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 1927 Ambedkar had decided to launch active movements against untouchability. He led a satyagraha at Mahad for the right of untouchables to draw water from the town's main water tank. At a conference in late 1927 he condemned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manusmriti">Manusmriti</a> for justifying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> discrimination, and on 25 December he led thousands of followers in burning copies of it. In 1930 he launched the Kalaram Temple movement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a>, in which about 15,000 volunteers marched to the temple gates, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> authorities closed.</p><p>In 1932 the British government's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communal-award">Communal Award</a> announced a separate electorate for the "Depressed Classes". <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, who feared it would divide the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> community, fasted in Yerwada jail. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poona-pact">Poona Pact</a> of 24 September 1932, signed by Ambedkar and Madan Mohan Malaviya among 23 signatories, gave reserved seats within the general electorate, 148 in the provincial legislatures instead of the 71 of the Communal Award.</p><p>Ambedkar published Annihilation of Caste on 15 May 1936 and founded the Independent Labour Party, which won 11 of the 13 reserved and 3 of the 4 general seats it contested in 1937. He tabled a bill against the khoti system in Konkan and served on the Viceroy's Executive Council as minister of labour. After independence he became Law Minister and chairman of the Drafting Committee of the Constitution, which was adopted on 26 November 1949 and included reservations in civil services, schools and colleges for scheduled castes and tribes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-marx.jpg" alt="Karl Marx" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Marx_001_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Jabez Edwin Mayall, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ambedkar's opponents included orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> leaders, whom Annihilation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a> strongly criticised, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, whom the book rebuked. Gandhi fiercely opposed a separate electorate for untouchables, and in a 1955 BBC interview Ambedkar accused him of writing against the caste system in English-language papers while supporting it in Gujarati ones. He also accused Nehru of being "conscious of the fact that he is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a>". His claim that Hinduism was the foundation of the caste system made him controversial and unpopular among Hindus.</p><p>He was no ally of the Communists, of whom he said in 1938: "I am an implacable enemy of the Communists." The Marxist theorist Abhinav Sinha replied that Ambedkar had not read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> or Lenin. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> or The Partition of India (1940) he argued that attempts to forge Hindu-Muslim unity within one state would lead to continuous conflict, and he accepted that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> should be redrawn with compulsory population exchanges. He also criticised child marriage and the treatment of women in Muslim society.</p><p>Critics have faulted him for one-sided views on caste at the expense of cooperation with the larger nationalist movement, and some biographers for neglecting organisation-building. In 1953 he told parliament that he had been a "hack" on the constitution, that he had acted "much against my will" and that he would be "the first person to burn it out". His Riddles in Hinduism, published after his death, led to mass demonstrations and counter-demonstrations.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;B. R. Ambedkar&#8221; cites where it covers Opposition to untouchability:</p><ul><li>Christophe Jaffrelot, Dr Ambedkar and Untouchability: Analysing and Fighting Caste (C. Hurst &amp; Co. Publishers, 2005)</li><li>Sukhadeo Thorat, B. R. Ambedkar:perspectives on social exclusion and inclusive policies (Oxford University Press, 2008)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ramabai died in 1935 after a long illness. At the Yeola Conversion Conference on 13 October that year Ambedkar announced his intention to convert to another religion, and he said that he had been born a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> but would not die one. He considered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a> but concluded that he might get "second-rate" status there. Around 1950 he turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> for a meeting of the World Fellowship of Buddhists and visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> twice in 1954. He founded the Bharatiya Bauddha Mahasabha in 1955.</p><p>On 27 September 1951 he resigned from Nehru's cabinet after the Hindu code bill was defeated. He lost the 1952 election in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> North to his former assistant Narayan Sadoba Kajrolkar, and placed third in the 1954 by-election at Bhandara. He married Sharada Kabir, a doctor, on 15 April 1948, and she took the name Savita Ambedkar. He had diabetes from 1948 and spent June to October 1954 in bed.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> on 14 October 1956 he accepted the Three Refuges and Five Precepts from a Buddhist monk and then converted some 500,000 of his supporters, for whom he prescribed 22 Vows. He died in his sleep in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> on 6 December 1956, three days after completing The Buddha and His Dhamma. Half a million people attended his cremation at Dadar Chowpatty beach, and no ministers attended. Further conversions took place there on 16 December.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amartya-sen.jpg" alt="Amartya Sen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen">Amartya Sen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amartya_Sen_20071128_cologne_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elke Wetzig (Elya); derivative work by ChaudhryAzan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ambedkar's political philosophy has given rise to many political parties, publications and workers' unions across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. His conversion renewed interest in Buddhist philosophy in India and abroad, and later mass conversion ceremonies have followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> example of 1956. Some Indian Buddhists regard him as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">Bodhisattva</a>, which he never claimed himself. In the late 1990s some Hungarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani people</a>, inspired by him, began to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. The Bharatiya Bauddha Mahasabha he founded was led after him by his son Yashwant Ambedkar.</p><p>His birthday, Ambedkar Jayanti, is a public holiday in many Indian states, and he received the Bharat Ratna posthumously in 1990. His bronze statue was installed in the Parliament of India in 1967. Scholar Anand Teltumbde noted that in the Congress's years of monopoly he was neglected, and that ten years after his death there was no road or monument in his name. Steven Calabresi, agreeing with Martha Nussbaum, has called the constitution "Ambedkar's constitution" for the purposive approach the Supreme Court later took, which Ambedkar had supported in a minority.</p><p>The economist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen">Amartya Sen</a> has called him the "father of my economics", and Narendra Jadhav described him as the highest educated Indian economist of all times. Ambedkar himself, in his concluding speech of 25 November 1949, said the credit for the constitution belonged partly to Sir B. N. Rau, who prepared its rough draft. His salutation "Jai Bhim" and the title Babasaheb are used by his followers.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>B. R. Ambedkar (1891–1956), born into a Mahar family treated as untouchable, studied at Columbia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> School of Economics and led campaigns against untouchability. He chaired the drafting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a> and in 1956 converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> with some 500,000 followers.</em></p>

